1
00:00:04,678 --> 00:00:15,609
[SPEAKER_01]: Wanting of sensual objects, or rather, wanting of objects that you derive pleasure from on the level of senses, that's what sensuality is.

2
00:00:16,190 --> 00:00:17,171
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that wanting.

3
00:00:19,093 --> 00:00:27,482
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in order to abandon sensuality, you have to stop being concerned with the objects that you want.

4
00:00:28,897 --> 00:00:34,762
[SPEAKER_01]: And start developing not wanting of that wanting of the objects, of senses.

5
00:00:36,784 --> 00:00:45,651
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, restraint needs to be done first, and then on the basis of that, you can start discerning the nature of wanting of anything pretty much sensual.

6
00:00:46,271 --> 00:00:51,696
[SPEAKER_01]: Not this... well, manifested through this or that, but overall it aims at anything that will give you the sensual pleasure.

7
00:00:52,697 --> 00:00:54,098
[SPEAKER_01]: Sight, sound, smell, taste, touches.

8
00:00:55,502 --> 00:01:03,748
[SPEAKER_01]: But for as long as you just... if you remain just focused with the objects that you want, you're failing to see the root of that sensuality.

9
00:01:06,110 --> 00:01:15,496
[SPEAKER_01]: But, consequently, if you develop not-wanting in regard to wanting of sense-objects, that's how you also overcome the sense-objects.

10
00:01:15,936 --> 00:01:23,662
[SPEAKER_01]: You lose concern with them, because you uprooted them, through developing not-wanting of their wanting.

11
00:01:25,509 --> 00:01:28,051
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sensuality is the wanting of sensual objects.

12
00:01:28,611 --> 00:01:33,955
[SPEAKER_01]: As the Buddha would say, he welcomes sensuality, entertains it, delights in it.

13
00:01:34,075 --> 00:01:39,299
[SPEAKER_01]: There would be you wanting and accepting that wanting of sensual objects at face value.

14
00:01:41,881 --> 00:01:52,409
[SPEAKER_01]: So, a skilled monk, a person who practices correctly, will then first be restrained, of course, and then start seeing that it's that wanting that's the problem.

15
00:01:55,469 --> 00:01:56,530
[SPEAKER_01]: Not the sensual objects.

16
00:01:56,850 --> 00:01:57,831
[SPEAKER_01]: Not the sensual objects.

17
00:01:57,931 --> 00:02:01,013
[SPEAKER_01]: Wanting is in regard to... it's wanting of the sensual objects.

18
00:02:01,374 --> 00:02:05,537
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what he should not welcome, entertain, delight in.

19
00:02:05,977 --> 00:02:16,086
[SPEAKER_01]: So, through not welcoming, entertaining and delighting the wanting of the sensual objects, you surmount the whole domain of sensuality, which is sensual objects and wanting of them.

20
00:02:18,628 --> 00:02:24,332
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean when I say, develop the not-wanting of wanting of sensual objects.

21
00:02:24,352 --> 00:02:24,912
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what it means.

22
00:02:24,932 --> 00:02:25,613
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't delight.

23
00:02:26,173 --> 00:02:26,993
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't entertain.

24
00:02:27,354 --> 00:02:30,236
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't welcome the wanting of sensual objects.

25
00:02:32,317 --> 00:02:43,764
[SPEAKER_01]: And that wanting of sensual objects, eh... See, a person who doesn't see this, will be either concerned with sensual objects, denying them, telling them, oh, it's ugly, it's ugly, it's impermanent, it's impermanent.

26
00:02:43,784 --> 00:02:47,507
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, eh... miss... missupplying his efforts.

27
00:02:52,105 --> 00:02:53,245
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the problem.

28
00:02:53,486 --> 00:02:54,707
[SPEAKER_01]: They themselves are not problem.

29
00:02:55,307 --> 00:03:01,611
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, equally, he then... he'll try to override that initial wanting of sensual objects.

30
00:03:01,812 --> 00:03:02,752
[SPEAKER_01]: Try to get rid of it.

31
00:03:03,433 --> 00:03:04,934
[SPEAKER_01]: Try to prevent it from arising.

32
00:03:05,314 --> 00:03:06,235
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't do that.

33
00:03:07,040 --> 00:03:08,100
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's not on you.

34
00:03:08,780 --> 00:03:12,081
[SPEAKER_01]: As the Buddha said, the eye wants to see the pleasant sights.

35
00:03:12,281 --> 00:03:14,501
[SPEAKER_01]: The ear, nose, smell...

36
00:03:14,741 --> 00:03:15,782
[SPEAKER_01]: Nose wants to smell.

37
00:03:15,882 --> 00:03:16,722
[SPEAKER_01]: Ear wants to hear.

38
00:03:16,822 --> 00:03:17,662
[SPEAKER_01]: Body wants to touch.

39
00:03:18,182 --> 00:03:19,842
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just the structure of your experience.

40
00:03:19,882 --> 00:03:20,923
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the five senses.

41
00:03:20,963 --> 00:03:21,823
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how they operate.

42
00:03:21,843 --> 00:03:23,863
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what we refer to as feeding grounds.

43
00:03:25,483 --> 00:03:31,605
[SPEAKER_01]: So, trying to interfere with that wanting of your sense objects... Is taking it up.

44
00:03:31,905 --> 00:03:32,985
[SPEAKER_01]: Is also taking it up.

45
00:03:33,025 --> 00:03:33,405
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

46
00:03:33,665 --> 00:03:34,045
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

47
00:03:34,572 --> 00:03:40,758
[SPEAKER_01]: So, and that's often people will misunderstand when Buddha said you don't delight, you don't welcome, don't entertain it.

48
00:03:41,058 --> 00:03:44,781
[SPEAKER_01]: Does not mean you deny that first arising.

49
00:03:44,801 --> 00:03:46,022
[SPEAKER_01]: Means you see it as a whole.

50
00:03:46,062 --> 00:03:49,786
[SPEAKER_01]: You see that there is the sense objects that are being wanted.

51
00:03:50,702 --> 00:03:51,982
[SPEAKER_01]: By the senses, basically.

52
00:03:52,383 --> 00:03:53,943
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what you don't delight in.

53
00:03:54,063 --> 00:03:55,043
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what you don't want.

54
00:03:55,403 --> 00:03:59,405
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't want to entertain the wanting of sensuality.

55
00:03:59,765 --> 00:04:08,467
[SPEAKER_01]: But, if wanting of sensuality has a reason, it's gonna persist on its own, as an arisen dhamma, as an arisen phenomena, for as long as it persists.

56
00:04:08,868 --> 00:04:16,830
[SPEAKER_01]: Your only concern is to not delight in it, not welcome it, not entertain it, for any time during that duration.

57
00:04:17,877 --> 00:04:22,201
[SPEAKER_01]: At any point in that duration, you always remain, no, but I don't want that.

58
00:04:23,222 --> 00:04:24,363
[SPEAKER_01]: It remains unwanted.

59
00:04:25,043 --> 00:04:28,527
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, somebody comes in front of your door and wants to come in.

60
00:04:28,987 --> 00:04:31,309
[SPEAKER_01]: But the only way to come in is if you welcome it and let it in.

61
00:04:32,370 --> 00:04:37,495
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in the beginning, you might feel awkward and pressured and nervous because it's there, it's in front of your door.

62
00:04:37,535 --> 00:04:37,835
[SPEAKER_01]: What if?

63
00:04:37,915 --> 00:04:38,175
[SPEAKER_01]: What if?

64
00:04:38,195 --> 00:04:38,515
[SPEAKER_01]: What if?

65
00:04:39,291 --> 00:04:43,674
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you either go out, or you welcome them out of the weakness, or you engage with one way or the other.

66
00:04:44,214 --> 00:04:46,476
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you realize, well, wait a minute, it can't come in.

67
00:04:46,536 --> 00:04:49,077
[SPEAKER_01]: It can stand there for hundreds of years if it wants.

68
00:04:49,538 --> 00:04:50,598
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not my concern.

69
00:04:51,099 --> 00:04:53,080
[SPEAKER_01]: My concern is to not welcome it inside.

70
00:04:53,220 --> 00:04:53,580
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

71
00:04:54,101 --> 00:04:54,441
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

72
00:04:55,862 --> 00:04:59,504
[SPEAKER_01]: And, surprise, surprise, if you start practicing like that, it will go away on its own.

73
00:04:59,864 --> 00:05:01,105
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like the Mara's daughters.

74
00:05:01,485 --> 00:05:08,330
[SPEAKER_01]: They're gonna try and come and harass you for as long as they perceive a way in, a possibility of being allowed to come inside.

75
00:05:09,832 --> 00:05:13,915
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's when they tried with the Buddha, after his enlightenment, and there was nothing.

76
00:05:14,095 --> 00:05:14,695
[SPEAKER_01]: They couldn't get in.

77
00:05:14,775 --> 00:05:15,896
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the Mahārāja told them off.

78
00:05:16,777 --> 00:05:19,259
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, oh, I've been trying for years and you think you're gonna...

79
00:05:19,659 --> 00:05:30,967
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah, for those who don't practice like that, it's enough to just stand in front of the door and the person will cave in and start to engage out of whatever pressure and uncomfortable or whatever.

80
00:05:31,667 --> 00:05:35,590
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you recognize that, no, all I have to do is keep the door closed.

81
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:41,042
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like I try to chase them away, because that would be engaging with them.

82
00:05:41,582 --> 00:05:44,083
[SPEAKER_01]: Affirming or denying, either way, you engage with it.

83
00:05:44,663 --> 00:05:53,667
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only way to engage is... not engage, sorry, is to allow it to arise, whichever way it wants to arise, and then not welcome it.

84
00:05:54,848 --> 00:05:55,568
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't open the door.

85
00:05:55,828 --> 00:05:56,589
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't entertain it.

86
00:05:56,749 --> 00:05:57,509
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't engage with it.

87
00:05:58,770 --> 00:06:06,313
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you do that to the extent necessary, as in for the sufficient amount of time, it will stop coming.

88
00:06:09,802 --> 00:06:16,106
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how the other suttas we talked about and read, like, you establish your mind in the imperturbable, in regard to sensuality.

89
00:06:18,527 --> 00:06:23,430
[SPEAKER_01]: Because whether you're sight, sound, smell, taste, touch, whether it's like this, whether it's past, future, present, doesn't matter.

90
00:06:23,750 --> 00:06:25,291
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not concerned with the content of it.

91
00:06:26,152 --> 00:06:31,134
[SPEAKER_01]: You just maintain the attitude of not wanting the arisen wanting of these sense objects.

92
00:06:32,535 --> 00:06:32,936
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

93
00:06:37,118 --> 00:06:38,719
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you do that sufficient amount of time,

94
00:06:39,907 --> 00:06:46,150
[SPEAKER_01]: The arising of the wanting of sense objects, it's going to change its significance.

95
00:06:48,011 --> 00:06:49,992
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not going to be about sense objects anymore.

96
00:06:50,012 --> 00:06:53,213
[SPEAKER_01]: It's going to be about, oh, this is that which is not welcomed.

97
00:06:53,813 --> 00:06:55,514
[SPEAKER_01]: This is that which is not delighted in.

98
00:06:56,034 --> 00:06:57,495
[SPEAKER_01]: This is that which is not wanted.

99
00:06:58,135 --> 00:07:01,077
[SPEAKER_01]: This is that which used to be my sensuality, basically.

100
00:07:04,318 --> 00:07:05,679
[SPEAKER_00]: This used to mean...

101
00:07:06,648 --> 00:07:10,889
[SPEAKER_01]: It used to mean, go after these objects, because they are wanted, and you took that at a face value.

102
00:07:11,229 --> 00:07:12,269
[SPEAKER_01]: Or don't go after these things.

103
00:07:12,629 --> 00:07:16,850
[SPEAKER_01]: Or don't go after these things, because they're not wanted, and you took that at a face value.

104
00:07:18,491 --> 00:07:20,671
[SPEAKER_01]: Or rather, you wanted the absence of these things.

105
00:07:21,031 --> 00:07:21,292
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

106
00:07:21,592 --> 00:07:23,272
[SPEAKER_01]: And you took that wanting at a face value.

107
00:07:23,992 --> 00:07:30,094
[SPEAKER_01]: But now, you stop welcoming, stop entertaining, you stop wanting the wanting of the sense objects.

108
00:07:32,274 --> 00:07:35,535
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it, it, the significant change, changes.

109
00:07:36,144 --> 00:07:38,745
[SPEAKER_01]: While the objects remain the same, the whole domain remains the same.

110
00:07:39,285 --> 00:07:43,948
[SPEAKER_01]: The eyes still have their feeding grounds, but now you conquer them, as the Buddha would say.

111
00:07:44,088 --> 00:07:46,589
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, they can't go and feed as wild animals used to do.

112
00:07:47,429 --> 00:07:48,490
[SPEAKER_01]: You are the one in control.

113
00:07:49,710 --> 00:07:54,713
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the eye will get to eat only what the mind, who doesn't want any of it, allows it.

114
00:07:55,373 --> 00:07:59,535
[SPEAKER_01]: And whatever it allows, it won't be sensual thing, because that has been abandoned.

115
00:08:00,755 --> 00:08:04,117
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensuality requires taking of that wanting for granted.

116
00:08:05,177 --> 00:08:12,719
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, if you stop wanting the wanting of sense-objects, you get to see the extent of that wanting in regard to the sense-objects.

117
00:08:13,059 --> 00:08:15,900
[SPEAKER_01]: You get to understand, as the Guru would say, gratification of it.

118
00:08:16,180 --> 00:08:18,501
[SPEAKER_01]: The extent, how far it can provide for you.

119
00:08:19,721 --> 00:08:24,022
[SPEAKER_01]: By seeing that, you get to understand the danger of it.

120
00:08:24,122 --> 00:08:30,444
[SPEAKER_01]: By not wanting the wanting of sense-objects, you understood the danger liable within wanting of the sense-objects.

121
00:08:37,658 --> 00:08:46,002
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, say, you say yes to the wanting of pleasant sights, for example, and within next 10 seconds you go blind.

122
00:08:46,022 --> 00:08:48,603
[SPEAKER_01]: Or those sights are, like, quickly ripped away from you.

123
00:08:50,624 --> 00:08:52,625
[SPEAKER_00]: Pleasing person or whatever.

124
00:08:54,126 --> 00:08:54,926
[SPEAKER_01]: Would you be affected?

125
00:08:54,986 --> 00:08:55,706
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, you would.

126
00:08:56,207 --> 00:09:02,329
[SPEAKER_01]: But, if you never given in to the original wanting of those sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, would you be affected?

127
00:09:03,050 --> 00:09:03,430
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't.

128
00:09:05,212 --> 00:09:07,073
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you never took on that wanting.

129
00:09:07,293 --> 00:09:08,434
[SPEAKER_01]: You never welcomed it in.

130
00:09:10,835 --> 00:09:25,063
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the simile of that mango tree, where a man climbs the mango tree to eat the ripe mangoes, but then another man comes and also sees the mangoes, doesn't see the other man who is eating up at the top, but he can't climb the tree, so he decides to cut the tree down.

131
00:09:25,563 --> 00:09:30,546
[SPEAKER_01]: He decides to take those sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches for himself, whichever way he deemed appropriate.

132
00:09:31,340 --> 00:09:39,362
[SPEAKER_01]: And if that... the other man who climbed the tree doesn't come down quickly, he's going to be destroyed or hurt, injured, killed or whatever.

133
00:09:40,982 --> 00:09:42,202
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the nature of sensuality.

134
00:09:42,742 --> 00:09:52,764
[SPEAKER_01]: That directionality of wanting the sense objects is always directional, as in, it's always going to take you one way or the other, which means you can't maintain the perspective on the domain.

135
00:09:53,304 --> 00:09:57,505
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to commit to it and go with it, which then means you are not in control.

136
00:09:58,593 --> 00:10:02,875
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's all well and good where the things are going the way you kind of wish them to go.

137
00:10:03,875 --> 00:10:06,716
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's inconceivable that that's how they're gonna always be.

138
00:10:08,337 --> 00:10:17,121
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just from the side of your own senses failing, or could fail, or be affected by accidents, injuries and so on, illnesses, but also the objects as well.

139
00:10:17,721 --> 00:10:18,421
[SPEAKER_01]: Millions of times.

140
00:10:18,782 --> 00:10:20,482
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is more?

141
00:10:20,602 --> 00:10:21,263
[SPEAKER_01]: Just ask yourself.

142
00:10:21,283 --> 00:10:21,803
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is more?

143
00:10:22,536 --> 00:10:25,458
[SPEAKER_01]: The times I got what I want, or the times I didn't get what I want?

144
00:10:26,218 --> 00:10:28,800
[SPEAKER_01]: And you realize, of course, it's the times I didn't get what I want.

145
00:10:29,500 --> 00:10:32,021
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whenever I got what I want, it was kind of almost like, oh, wow.

146
00:10:33,762 --> 00:10:34,083
[SPEAKER_01]: Lucky.

147
00:10:34,763 --> 00:10:35,343
[SPEAKER_01]: Accidental.

148
00:10:35,523 --> 00:10:36,244
[SPEAKER_01]: Circumstantial.

149
00:10:36,704 --> 00:10:39,105
[SPEAKER_01]: On the basis of, more often, not getting what I want.

150
00:10:39,245 --> 00:10:39,826
[SPEAKER_01]: How I want it.

151
00:10:41,487 --> 00:10:46,830
[SPEAKER_01]: But, if you stop... if you stop wanting of any type of wanting of the sense objects,

152
00:10:48,557 --> 00:10:53,940
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot not get what you want, because you only want one thing.

153
00:10:53,960 --> 00:10:54,801
[SPEAKER_01]: Non-sensuality.

154
00:10:56,221 --> 00:10:57,862
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's now implied in sensuality.

155
00:10:58,683 --> 00:11:02,585
[SPEAKER_01]: So sensuality keeps reminding you of your freedom of sensuality.

156
00:11:04,266 --> 00:11:07,228
[SPEAKER_01]: Sense objects keeps reminding you of your imperturbable state.

157
00:11:10,590 --> 00:11:16,033
[SPEAKER_00]: You can get that not wanting of sensuality instantly.

158
00:11:17,909 --> 00:11:20,270
[SPEAKER_00]: What do you mean by that?

159
00:11:21,111 --> 00:11:23,572
[SPEAKER_01]: If you see it on the right level, sure, yeah.

160
00:11:23,852 --> 00:11:26,873
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't get no wanting of sensuality just because you don't want sensuality.

161
00:11:27,814 --> 00:11:30,895
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you don't know what sensuality is to the full extent.

162
00:11:31,135 --> 00:11:34,577
[SPEAKER_01]: So, first, you need to understand what sensuality is, which is what I explained, how we started this.

163
00:11:35,037 --> 00:11:39,099
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the wanting of the sense objects, not just the sense objects or just the wanting.

164
00:11:40,113 --> 00:11:43,694
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the wanting of these pleasing sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches.

165
00:11:44,094 --> 00:11:48,615
[SPEAKER_01]: It's this pressure that I experience on the level of my body, on the level of my senses.

166
00:11:49,655 --> 00:12:02,799
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you stop wanting that wanting, which means you have to be... you also have to stop being concerned with the objects of your sensuality, but start looking at the nature of sensuality, which is it's being wanted.

167
00:12:03,679 --> 00:12:07,580
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't want that which is being wanted in regard to the

168
00:12:12,699 --> 00:12:23,949
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, then you can say, I don't want centrality correctly, because you know what centrality is.

169
00:12:23,969 --> 00:12:25,411
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, what do you get instead?

170
00:12:26,812 --> 00:12:27,633
[SPEAKER_01]: Unshakable peace.

171
00:12:30,215 --> 00:12:31,056
[SPEAKER_01]: Freedom from suffering.

172
00:12:33,798 --> 00:12:35,760
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is... Imperturbable.

173
00:12:35,800 --> 00:12:36,401
[SPEAKER_00]: State of the mind.

174
00:12:36,741 --> 00:12:38,202
[SPEAKER_00]: Inconceivable in some ways.

175
00:12:39,451 --> 00:12:45,073
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's inconceivable from the point of view of the central domain.

176
00:12:46,173 --> 00:12:53,175
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if the mind still takes centrality for granted, doesn't see the extent of the central domain, the domain of imperturbable is inconceivable.

177
00:12:55,475 --> 00:13:08,099
[SPEAKER_01]: But if the mind starts paying attention to the domain of centrality, discerning it, and by doing so, discerning the domain that's free from it, the domain of imperturbable, then it becomes conceivable, it becomes doable.

178
00:13:09,528 --> 00:13:10,169
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you understood.

179
00:13:10,469 --> 00:13:13,472
[SPEAKER_01]: Because sensuality, inherently, in itself, is unpleasant.

180
00:13:13,873 --> 00:13:15,154
[SPEAKER_01]: It's dangerous.

181
00:13:15,194 --> 00:13:16,936
[SPEAKER_01]: It's of very little gratification.

182
00:13:17,016 --> 00:13:19,840
[SPEAKER_01]: Not worth even making the effort for that gratification.

183
00:13:22,643 --> 00:13:30,351
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like somebody asking you to pay the lottery ticket $1,000 and the maximum win is $5.

184
00:13:32,147 --> 00:13:33,808
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you... why would you do that?

185
00:13:33,988 --> 00:13:34,669
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't do that.

186
00:13:34,949 --> 00:13:35,590
[SPEAKER_01]: It's ridiculous.

187
00:13:36,190 --> 00:13:37,171
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what sensuality is.

188
00:13:37,931 --> 00:13:46,857
[SPEAKER_01]: The amount of reward you get to the utmost, most refined sensuality you can imagine, is hundreds of your investment, always, every single time.

189
00:13:47,878 --> 00:13:54,523
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha gave all those similes like a bone not providing anything, but a dog can't stop chewing it, because he's just mad with it.

190
00:13:55,664 --> 00:13:57,305
[SPEAKER_01]: But you get nothing in return.

191
00:13:57,625 --> 00:14:00,167
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the definition of sensuality, of sensual domain.

192
00:14:01,070 --> 00:14:02,851
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's of little gratification.

193
00:14:02,991 --> 00:14:03,291
[SPEAKER_01]: It is.

194
00:14:03,331 --> 00:14:09,114
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because you romantically pondered over it and you mused how unsatisfactory.

195
00:14:09,154 --> 00:14:09,254
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

196
00:14:09,354 --> 00:14:12,735
[SPEAKER_01]: Factually, that's the deal you get from sensuality.

197
00:14:14,116 --> 00:14:17,117
[SPEAKER_01]: For the thousand pieces you invested, you get one in return.

198
00:14:17,797 --> 00:14:18,798
[SPEAKER_01]: And you realize, wait a minute.

199
00:14:19,298 --> 00:14:23,180
[SPEAKER_01]: All I need to do is not invest in this and I'll gain thousandfold.

200
00:14:24,560 --> 00:14:28,242
[SPEAKER_01]: All I need to do is practice renunciation and I gain thousandfold.

201
00:14:29,342 --> 00:14:32,923
[SPEAKER_01]: All I need to do is not do sensuality, and I win.

202
00:14:35,383 --> 00:14:36,923
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the pleasure of jhāna as well.

203
00:14:37,443 --> 00:14:43,445
[SPEAKER_01]: That relief of burden, of pressure, of freedom from sensuality, freedom from danger of sensuality.

204
00:14:44,185 --> 00:14:51,446
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just that you invest a thousand, knowing, like, well, maybe not fully knowing, but in practice, you get only one in return, never more.

205
00:14:52,106 --> 00:14:54,546
[SPEAKER_01]: But even that, you can lose your life on account of it.

206
00:14:55,687 --> 00:14:57,247
[SPEAKER_01]: So, not just that you don't get any reward,

207
00:14:58,094 --> 00:14:59,695
[SPEAKER_01]: You actually get even taken even more.

208
00:14:59,975 --> 00:15:00,956
[SPEAKER_01]: You could lose even more.

209
00:15:01,757 --> 00:15:03,578
[SPEAKER_01]: More risk, yeah.

210
00:15:03,638 --> 00:15:04,459
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

211
00:15:05,339 --> 00:15:06,320
[SPEAKER_00]: But the other way...

212
00:15:06,740 --> 00:15:12,264
[SPEAKER_01]: The other way is risk-free, debt-free and loss-free.

213
00:15:12,885 --> 00:15:14,446
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like a perfect advertisement.

214
00:15:14,506 --> 00:15:15,246
[SPEAKER_01]: Practice jhāna.

215
00:15:17,528 --> 00:15:18,069
[SPEAKER_01]: But no, it is.

216
00:15:18,169 --> 00:15:18,409
[SPEAKER_01]: It is.

217
00:15:19,149 --> 00:15:25,374
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whenever the Buddha would say it's of little gratification, little piece of... Again, it's a factual thing that he would be describing.

218
00:15:26,562 --> 00:15:35,030
[SPEAKER_01]: And because sensuality is of little gratification, little satisfaction and much greater danger, that's why if you get to understand it, you will abandon it.

219
00:15:35,731 --> 00:15:39,755
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot understand the nature of sensuality correctly and still want it.

220
00:15:40,235 --> 00:15:41,637
[SPEAKER_01]: Two are mutually exclusive.

221
00:15:43,058 --> 00:15:45,701
[SPEAKER_01]: Understanding of it means not wanting it.

222
00:15:50,265 --> 00:15:57,066
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why we would say, the man who engages with sensuality, who seeks happiness, who wants happiness, who wants joy.

223
00:15:57,986 --> 00:16:02,187
[SPEAKER_01]: So, from this, like, that's why people engage with sensuality, because that's the only pleasure they know.

224
00:16:03,587 --> 00:16:05,548
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's not necessarily the problem.

225
00:16:05,828 --> 00:16:07,068
[SPEAKER_01]: You use the same criteria.

226
00:16:07,508 --> 00:16:08,208
[SPEAKER_01]: I want pleasure.

227
00:16:10,429 --> 00:16:11,469
[SPEAKER_01]: The senses want pleasure.

228
00:16:12,189 --> 00:16:12,669
[SPEAKER_01]: That's fine.

229
00:16:13,489 --> 00:16:15,830
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's what pleasure you pursue with it.

230
00:16:16,470 --> 00:16:17,290
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the difference.

231
00:16:18,892 --> 00:16:26,536
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's also where the Buddha realized, he said, wait a minute, the pleasure of jhāna, the pleasure of withdrawal, the pleasure of renunciation, there's nothing unwholesome about it.

232
00:16:26,556 --> 00:16:28,617
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why would I fear the pleasure in itself?

233
00:16:30,038 --> 00:16:32,760
[SPEAKER_01]: I won't fear this pleasure, because it's not unwholesome.

234
00:16:34,521 --> 00:16:36,842
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what the other suttas would say.

235
00:16:37,763 --> 00:16:40,024
[SPEAKER_01]: The man who wants pleasure would then reflect.

236
00:16:40,144 --> 00:16:42,465
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, because he wants pleasure, he would go to sensuality.

237
00:16:42,805 --> 00:16:43,626
[SPEAKER_01]: But then you reflect.

238
00:16:44,827 --> 00:16:46,367
[SPEAKER_01]: And you realize, wait a minute, I want pleasure.

239
00:16:46,507 --> 00:16:47,328
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is more pleasant?

240
00:16:48,052 --> 00:16:52,255
[SPEAKER_01]: Investing thousand pieces and getting one in return, maybe.

241
00:16:53,576 --> 00:16:56,698
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, refraining from investing anything and keeping it all.

242
00:16:57,799 --> 00:16:58,900
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, so that's more pleasant.

243
00:16:59,320 --> 00:17:06,325
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even from the point of view of wanting the pleasure of sensuality, you will get more through restraining that sensuality.

244
00:17:09,527 --> 00:17:12,409
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why sensuality and carelessness go hand in hand.

245
00:17:13,223 --> 00:17:18,406
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to be careless and lose the sight of this perspective in order to be pulled by centrality and give in.

246
00:17:20,667 --> 00:17:21,788
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's the starting point.

247
00:17:22,489 --> 00:17:26,091
[SPEAKER_01]: Everybody starts by being careless and being central, within the central domain.

248
00:17:26,991 --> 00:17:30,073
[SPEAKER_01]: So you start with a restraint in order to develop perspective on it.

249
00:17:30,273 --> 00:17:32,895
[SPEAKER_01]: When you develop perspective, then you can say no to the right things.

250
00:17:58,923 --> 00:18:04,945
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why what we said in the other talk, that sensuality is fundamentally not wanted by anyone.

251
00:18:08,386 --> 00:18:14,008
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, discerning that, you habitually just go with that arisen wanting of your senses.

252
00:18:14,789 --> 00:18:16,849
[SPEAKER_01]: But you appropriate it, and then you think you want it.

253
00:18:18,410 --> 00:18:20,731
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't... you don't deny the sense objects.

254
00:18:21,651 --> 00:18:23,952
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't deny the wanting of sense objects.

255
00:18:25,026 --> 00:18:30,350
[SPEAKER_01]: But you say no to it and you let it endure without giving in at any point of that duration.

256
00:18:31,650 --> 00:18:40,256
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you realize the pain here, the unpleasant aspect, is the aspect of sense... senses suffering on account of not getting the object.

257
00:18:40,817 --> 00:18:42,758
[SPEAKER_01]: But none of that... neither of that is mine.

258
00:18:43,676 --> 00:18:45,558
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, eventually, those senses will calm down.

259
00:18:45,918 --> 00:18:49,021
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the way I gave the simile of the five wild animals that you tame.

260
00:18:49,041 --> 00:19:01,632
[SPEAKER_01]: In the beginning, you just peg them onto like a... into a stake on the ground, and they're going to be pulling and ripping and yanking and chewing and trying to escape, until they burn all the energy, and then they just sit, and then they'll start to listen.

261
00:19:05,833 --> 00:19:20,558
[SPEAKER_01]: And if that's not working for somebody in someone's practice, it's because they're probably still trying to either deny the wanting of sense objects, as in interfere with it, or are too concerned with denying of the sense objects themselves.

262
00:19:20,578 --> 00:19:22,799
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, this is super, super, this is ugly, this is ugly, this is ugly.

263
00:19:27,580 --> 00:19:32,082
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, whichever way it is, they don't have the perspective on the nature of the sensual domain.

264
00:19:34,888 --> 00:19:37,209
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you do, then you realize, no, it has a reason on its own.

265
00:19:37,549 --> 00:19:38,469
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensuality is there.

266
00:19:38,870 --> 00:19:41,090
[SPEAKER_01]: Eyes want to see.

267
00:19:41,130 --> 00:19:42,551
[SPEAKER_01]: Nose wants to smell, and so on.

268
00:19:43,491 --> 00:19:44,372
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't interfere with that.

269
00:19:45,072 --> 00:19:45,852
[SPEAKER_01]: Just don't welcome it.

270
00:19:46,092 --> 00:19:46,753
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't entertain it.

271
00:19:47,973 --> 00:19:49,054
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't be careless in regard to it.

272
00:19:49,074 --> 00:19:50,974
[SPEAKER_01]: And they won't be dragging you off into all these places.

273
00:19:51,014 --> 00:19:52,075
[SPEAKER_00]: They can't.

274
00:19:52,435 --> 00:19:56,376
[SPEAKER_01]: Only through you giving into it, you get dragged.

275
00:19:56,436 --> 00:19:57,897
[SPEAKER_00]: But also the attitude that you're talking

276
00:20:02,412 --> 00:20:03,633
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, sure, sure, I see.

277
00:20:03,753 --> 00:20:04,414
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I see what you mean.

278
00:20:04,494 --> 00:20:05,074
[SPEAKER_01]: No, absolutely.

279
00:20:05,454 --> 00:20:11,919
[SPEAKER_01]: So, not wanting, the wanting of sense objects needs to be, well, needs to be universal.

280
00:20:13,440 --> 00:20:14,661
[SPEAKER_01]: That attitude needs to be developed.

281
00:20:14,962 --> 00:20:17,504
[SPEAKER_01]: This is not just for today and tomorrow that I don't want this.

282
00:20:18,264 --> 00:20:21,226
[SPEAKER_01]: This is for the rest of my life and any lives that might come.

283
00:20:24,049 --> 00:20:28,572
[SPEAKER_01]: The wanting of sense objects will never be wanted, will never be welcomed.

284
00:20:29,766 --> 00:20:32,186
[SPEAKER_01]: It might arise millions of times more for you.

285
00:20:33,307 --> 00:20:37,527
[SPEAKER_01]: Every time it arises, every time, it is not welcome and you don't open the door.

286
00:20:38,928 --> 00:20:41,948
[SPEAKER_01]: And eventually, it will have to go away.

287
00:20:41,968 --> 00:20:44,429
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, you will win.

288
00:20:46,129 --> 00:20:52,830
[SPEAKER_01]: You will win by not being bothered, by not losing your investment stupidly, by not exposing yourself to risk.

289
00:20:53,510 --> 00:20:57,091
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, on top of it, by discerning the nature of the imperturbable,

290
00:20:57,528 --> 00:21:01,111
[SPEAKER_01]: Which in itself, it's immeasurably more pleasurable in a wholesome sense.

291
00:21:02,493 --> 00:21:08,558
[SPEAKER_01]: Like the pleasure of the first jhāna, you can't even compare it to the pleasure of sensuality.

292
00:21:08,918 --> 00:21:12,301
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just incomparable, because two different domains, mutually exclusive.

293
00:21:13,142 --> 00:21:13,943
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't have...

294
00:21:13,963 --> 00:21:16,105
[SPEAKER_01]: I have jhānas and I have sensuality.

295
00:21:16,125 --> 00:21:18,727
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes, like, I engage sensuality in an establishment, I engage jhānas.

296
00:21:18,767 --> 00:21:20,669
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that's all sensuality, my friend.

297
00:21:23,462 --> 00:21:29,504
[SPEAKER_01]: Through discerning and abandoning of sensual domain, you discern the main imperturbable in regard to that domain.

298
00:21:29,744 --> 00:21:31,825
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the pleasure of the jhānas.

299
00:21:33,605 --> 00:21:35,086
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's just for this weekend.

300
00:21:35,606 --> 00:21:36,706
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, exactly.

301
00:21:36,786 --> 00:21:37,847
[SPEAKER_01]: Or just while I'm on the retreat.

302
00:21:37,867 --> 00:21:44,349
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll make the effort, but when I go back... No, which means that the future possibility of welcoming sensuality, you welcome that.

303
00:21:45,305 --> 00:21:49,186
[SPEAKER_01]: And by welcoming the possibility of sensuality, you are still fully engaged with...

304
00:21:49,206 --> 00:21:53,788
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, I'm not gonna let you in today, but we both know I'm gonna open this door tomorrow.

305
00:21:54,508 --> 00:21:56,929
[SPEAKER_01]: And you think this person will then go in the meantime?

306
00:21:56,969 --> 00:21:58,010
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course not.

307
00:21:58,350 --> 00:21:59,430
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, that's the whole point.

308
00:22:02,091 --> 00:22:04,552
[SPEAKER_01]: To not open the door so they leave and never come back.

309
00:22:05,433 --> 00:22:10,014
[SPEAKER_01]: But the only way they will do that is they really see... No, he will never open the door.

310
00:22:10,374 --> 00:22:14,216
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to convince the Mara's daughter... daughters and Mara himself

311
00:22:14,863 --> 00:22:17,869
[SPEAKER_01]: That the door will never be open for them ever again.

312
00:22:20,535 --> 00:22:21,477
[SPEAKER_01]: And how long that takes?

313
00:22:21,537 --> 00:22:23,161
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that depends on the power...

